# Crypto Engine DISABLED — mainline sun8i-ce cannot drive the H713 CE (bench-
# proven 2026-07-23). Enabling it registers every algorithm, then each fails its
# known-answer self-test. Wiring the stock's 2nd interrupt (SPI 74) fixes task
# completion, but the CE then rejects mainline's task descriptors: ciphers report
# "address invalid", AES/SHA "algorithm not supported" — a different descriptor
# FORMAT (the vendor two-register-bank block), not an IRQ/clock/addressing gap.
# No CE TRNG either. Re-enabling needs descriptor-level RE of the vendor
# allwinner,sunxi-ce driver (source unavailable); the A53 has ARMv8 AES/SHA
# (software crypto ~2 GB/s, faster than this CE) so there is no benefit.
# See docs/status.md and docs/roadmap.md.
# CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_SUN8I_CE is not set
CONFIG_DMA_CMA=y
CONFIG_PRINTK_TIME=y
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_FS=y
# Magic SysRq, in the SHIPPING config and not just the debug fragments.
#
# On 2026-08-22 a wedged rootfs cost a physical power cycle: the eMMC stopped
# answering, so reboot and systemctl -- external binaries on the dead
# filesystem -- could not run, /proc still worked but nothing that touched a
# path did, and `exec 3>/dev/watchdog0` did not reset the board because systemd
# already holds and pets the watchdog. That left no software path out.
#
# `echo b > /proc/sysrq-trigger` needs no filesystem, and neither does a UART
# BREAK (tools/serial/sysrq.py). Note the standing finding that sysrq did NOT
# rescue an earlier *hard* lockup still holds and is not contradicted here:
# with every CPU spinning interrupts-disabled nothing runs the handler. This is
# for the softer case, which is the one that actually cost a trip to the board
# -- systemd was alive and printing throughout.
#
# MAGIC_SYSRQ_SERIAL is what makes the BREAK path work; the empty sequence
# means BREAK alone arms it.
#
# Verified live on hardware 2026-08-22, not merely compiled in:
# `echo h > /proc/sysrq-trigger` prints the banner including reboot(b),
# show-blocked-tasks(w) and show-task-states(t) -- the three that would have
# resolved the wedge above -- and /proc/sys/kernel/sysrq reads 1, so this
# rootfs does not restrict the mask the way a stock Debian sysctl can.
CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ=y
CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ_SERIAL=y
CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ_DEFAULT_ENABLE=0x1
# Reboot-to-fastboot handoff over the RTC GP7 nvmem cell (see the RTC section and
# the reboot-mode node in sun50i-h713.dtsi). Replaces the earlier syscon window
# that overlapped the RTC register region.
CONFIG_NVMEM_REBOOT_MODE=y
